sprd-dmaengine-pcm: period events from an hrtimer, not the DMA interrupt that never comes

Based on kanoqwq/e5-linux@13b485c (kernel/patches/0013-pcm-period-timer.patch), the same driver in the
Rongyue E5's 5.15 tree, ported to the 5.4 audio_driver tree this project builds.

The AGCP DMA completion interrupt never reaches the GIC on these boards: both sprd_dma lines in
/proc/interrupts stay at 0 for a whole stream (docs/FINDINGS.md 24, "Where it actually stops"), so
sprd_pcm_dma_buf_done() never runs and nothing calls snd_pcm_period_elapsed(). Every ordinary ALSA client -
aplay, arecord, mu300-voice's tone and echo - then blocks on its first full buffer and gives up with EIO,
which is exactly the hw_ptr-stuck-after-one-burst picture measured here. The E5 has the same SoC family and
the same symptom, with or without the AGDSP running.

The position needs no interrupt - sprd_pcm_pointer() reads the live DMA address - so streams that want
period wakeups get them from an hrtimer at the period rate, and the DMA is always programmed without
interrupts, like the NO_PERIOD_WAKEUP streams Android's HAL opens. period_timer=0 (module parameter of
sprd-dmaengine-pcm) restores the interrupt path.

pm_splk_dma_prot becomes irqsave: sprd_pcm_pointer() takes it, and the tick reaches it from hardirq context
through snd_pcm_period_elapsed(); with a plain spin_lock() a tick landing on a CPU inside one of these
sections spins for ever (the soft lockup the E5's first ticker attempt died of). The tick is stopped with
hrtimer_try_to_cancel() in trigger (which can run inside the tick via xrun -> snd_pcm_stop) and
synchronously in hw_free and close, which release the DMA channels and free rtd.

Hostless scenes (FE_VOICE, the call itself) have no DMA and are not affected.

